title: Week 5 – A Trail of Blood
description: Being Baptist, week 5: a brief history of the Baptists, a line of believers who held the Bible's distinctives in every age, and the B.A.P.T.I.S.T. distinctives they held.
output: lessons/doctrine/doctrine-week-5-trail-of-blood-and-baptist-history.html

# Built from the Week 5 notes, the Timeline of Splits handout and infographic, the Revelation 17
# handout, and the Trail of Blood verses handout, which this deck replaces.

=== Introduction
--- A Trail of Blood [title]
overline: Being Baptist · Week 5
sub: A brief history of the Baptists: believers who held to the Bible in every age, often at the cost of their lives.
refs: Hebrews 11:36-38; Revelation 6:9-10
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- A line of believers, not a chain of churches
lead: The Trail of Blood follows a simple, observable truth: in every age since Christ there have been believers living faith to faith, holding the same gospel and the same Bible distinctives, and paying for it in blood.
panel: What history shows | An unbroken line of believers, saved by grace through faith, who held believer’s baptism, the authority of scripture and the independence of the local church, under different names in every century. [[Romans 1:17]]
panel-warn: What it does not prove | An unbroken chain of one church, of pastors laying hands on pastors back to the apostles. That claim (Landmarkism, the “bride” doctrine) builds a rigid system on more than history or scripture can show.
callout: We learn from this history; we are not married to a system. The line that matters is faith, and the Bible decides what a church is, not a chain of ordinations.

--- The gates of hell shall not prevail
big: Matthew 16:18 | mark: the gates of hell shall not prevail against it
p: Christ promised His church would endure. He also promised it would suffer. The world has always hated those who hold His word without compromise. [[John 15:20]] [[2 Timothy 3:12]]
big: Revelation 17:6 | mark: drunken with the blood of the saints

=== The trail
--- The early church
step: 33–100 | **Apostolic Era.** The church founded by Jesus Christ spreads by apostolic preaching. Believers, first called Christians at Antioch, practice believer’s baptism and local church autonomy, persecuted by Jewish and Roman authorities for holding to scripture. [[Acts 11:26]]
step: 100–313 | **Post-apostolic persecutions.** Groups like the Montanists (c. 150) hold to biblical purity against an emerging hierarchy, under Roman persecution, and work to put Scripture in the common tongue.

--- The rise of the imperial church
step: 313 | **The Edict of Milan.** Constantine legalizes Christianity, and the state church that follows persecutes the churches that will not submit to it, like the Donatists (300s), who reject infant baptism and the hierarchy.
step: 313–600 | Rome comes to restrict Scripture to the Latin Vulgate and suppress the common tongue. Dissenters face death, exile, and the burning of their writings.
callout: The same pattern repeats for a thousand years: the Bible in the people’s own language, believer’s baptism, and no master but Christ, answered with the sword. [[Acts 5:29]]

--- Medieval dissenters
step: 600–1200 | **Paulicians and Bogomils.** They keep believer’s baptism and Scripture-based worship, through Catholic crusades and executions. Councils condemn Scripture in any tongue but Latin, and the translations are burned.
step: 1200–1500 | **Waldensians and Albigenses.** Peter Waldo’s followers (c. 1173) and the Cathars preach biblical simplicity and put the Bible into the common tongue. The papal bull *Ad Abolendam* (1184) orders them put down.
stat: 1209 | Béziers: up to 20,000 killed in one day
stat: 1209–29 | the Albigensian Crusade: hundreds of thousands killed
stat: 1545 | Mérindol: Waldensian villages massacred

--- Anabaptists and the Reformation
lead: Anabaptists (c. 1525) revive believer’s baptism and separation from the state church, and are persecuted by Catholics and Protestants alike.
stat: 1527 | Felix Manz drowned in Zürich
stat: 1529 | George Blaurock burned
stat: 1536 | William Tyndale strangled and burned
p: Tyndale gave England the Bible in English, and his books were burned under Rome’s decrees. Under Queen Mary (1553–1558) more than 280 were burned at the stake, John Rogers, Hugh Latimer and Nicholas Ridley among them. The French Wars of Religion (1562–1598), with the St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre (1572), took up to three million lives.

--- Baptists by name
step: 1609 | **John Smyth** forms a Baptist congregation of exiles in Amsterdam; English Baptists follow, under Anglican persecution, their preachers jailed and their Bibles burned.
step: 1651 | **John Clarke and Obadiah Holmes** are fined, and Holmes is whipped in Boston, for preaching Baptist principles in Puritan Massachusetts.
step: 1655 | **The Piedmont Easter** massacre of the Waldensians, in the Savoyard wars (1655–1690).
step: 1660s–80s | Virginia’s Anglican laws fine and jail Baptists for preaching without a license.

--- Liberty
lead: Persecution drove believers to the American colonies, where the Pilgrims landed in 1620. In time, the Baptist conviction of soul liberty became the nation’s.
- The Great Awakening (1730s–1740s) spread the gospel, and unlicensed preachers were arrested for it.
- The new states, many settled by those fleeing state churches, adopted the Baptist principle of religious liberty: the First Amendment forbids a state church.
- With that freedom, the gospel spread and Baptist churches formed throughout the nation.
refs: Romans 14:12; Galatians 5:1

--- The old paths
big: Jeremiah 6:16 | mark: ask for the old paths
p: Today Baptists keep the apostolic faith in local churches, with the King James Bible, against secularism, modernism and denominationalism. Not a new movement, and not an institution, but the old paths of the Bible. [[Proverbs 22:28]]

=== A history of division
--- Three great splits
step: 313 | **The Edict of Milan.** The state-aligned imperial church, later the Roman Catholic Church, parts from the independent believers over state control and practices like infant baptism.
step: 1054 | **The Great Schism.** The Latin West (Pope Leo IX) and the Greek East (Patriarch Michael Cerularius) divide over doctrine (the *filioque* clause), authority and culture: Catholic and Orthodox.
step: 1517 | **The Reformation.** Luther’s 95 Theses protest indulgences and preach faith alone and scripture alone.
note: The believers who never joined Rome were never part of these splits.

--- The Reformation’s contradiction
panel-warn: Lutheran (1517) | Faith alone, yet infant baptism kept as a means of grace.
panel-warn: Reformed (1520s) | Zwingli and Calvin: faith alone, yet infant baptism as a sign of the covenant.
panel-warn: Anglican (1534) | Henry VIII’s Church of England, its infant baptism and sacraments kept.
panel: Anabaptists (1525) | Grebel and Simons: believer’s baptism by immersion, no state church. The consistent answer: if salvation is by faith, baptism follows faith. [[Acts 8:36-37]]
big: Ephesians 2:8-9

--- Later divisions
panel: 1600s | Presbyterians (John Knox, rule by elders) and Congregationalists (local autonomy) from the Reformed, both keeping infant baptism.
panel: 1700s | Methodists from the Church of England (John Wesley, personal holiness), and the Quakers (George Fox, the “inner light”).
panel: 1800s | Adventists from the Millerites (1844), and the Holiness movement from the Methodists, with its “second blessing.”
panel: 1900s | Pentecostals from the Holiness movement (Azusa Street, 1906), and the Charismatic renewal spreading tongues across the denominations (1960s).

--- New religions
panel-warn: Latter-day Saints (1830) | Joseph Smith claims new revelation, the Book of Mormon.
panel-warn: Seventh-day Adventists (1844) | Ellen G. White, after the “Great Disappointment”: the Saturday Sabbath.
panel-warn: Jehovah’s Witnesses (1870s) | Charles Taze Russell: a “strict monotheism” that denies Christ is God.
big: Galatians 1:8

--- Where the Baptists come from
lead: Independent Baptists did not come out of the Reformation. They are the latest in a line of believers who never joined Rome, holding the same distinctives under many names.
step: Every age | Believers holding believer’s baptism, the local church and Scripture alone, apart from the state churches
step: 1600s | Baptist churches by name, in England and the colonies
step: 1800s | Independent Baptist churches, rejecting liberalism and sacramentalism
step: 1900s | Independent Fundamental Baptist churches: the Bible, the gospel of grace, believer’s baptism, local church autonomy
note: A family likeness of doctrine, not a line of succession.

=== Mystery, Babylon
--- The woman on the beast
scripture: Revelation 17:1-6 | mark: MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF HARLOTS; drunken with the blood of the saints
p: The great whore sits on seven mountains and many waters: peoples, multitudes, nations and tongues, drunk with the blood of the martyrs. The trail of blood leads straight to her door. [[Revelation 17:9]] [[Revelation 17:15]] [[Revelation 17:18]]

--- Mother of harlots
panel-warn: The city on seven hills | Rome, and the false church that rules from her, given power by the kings of the earth.
panel-warn: Her daughters | The churches that came out of her and kept her sacraments, her infant baptism and her hierarchy.
panel: Come out of her | God calls His people out, to be separate. [[Revelation 18:4]] [[2 Corinthians 6:17]]

=== Being Baptist
--- The B.A.P.T.I.S.T. distinctives
tile: B | **Biblical authority.** The Bible alone is our rule of faith and practice. [[2 Timothy 3:16-17]]
tile: A | **Autonomy of the local church.** Churches, plural: each self-governing under Christ. [[Acts 9:31]] [[Revelation 1:4]]
tile: P | **Priesthood of the believer.** Every believer comes to God through Christ alone. [[1 Peter 2:9]] [[1 Timothy 2:5]]
tile: T | **Two ordinances.** Baptism and the Lord’s Supper. [[Matthew 28:19]] [[1 Corinthians 11:23-26]]
tile: I | **Individual soul liberty.** Every one shall give account of himself to God. [[Romans 14:12]]
tile: S | **Saved church membership.** Believers, baptized after they are saved. [[Acts 2:41]]
tile: T | **Two offices.** Pastors and deacons. [[Philippians 1:1]]

--- What the distinctives mean
- The New Testament says “churches,” plural, 49 times: each one local and independent. [[Acts 9:31]]
- The Pastoral Epistles give the qualifications for **pastors and deacons**. [[1 Timothy 3:1-13]]
- **Bishop, elder, overseer and shepherd** are the same office: the pastor. [[Titus 1:5-7]] [[1 Peter 5:1-2]]
- The offices are not priests: **all believers are**. [[Revelation 1:6]]
- An **ordinance** is a practice the Lord commanded. It is not a “sacrament” that gives grace.
- “Anabaptist” means “baptize again,” a Catholic insult: the converts had been baptized as babies, and were baptized again as believers.

=== Behold, hear, declare
--- Behold, hear, set thine heart
big: Ezekiel 40:4 | mark: behold with thine eyes, and hear with thine ears, and set thine heart; declare all that thou seest
p: The believers on the trail did what Ezekiel was told: they looked into the word, heard it, kept it in their hearts, and declared it, in the common tongue, whatever it cost.
refs: Deuteronomy 11:18; Proverbs 4:20-21

--- Ears that will not hear
big: Acts 28:27
p: Paul quotes Isaiah. Many will not hear, as many would not in every age of the trail. The word is declared anyway. [[Isaiah 6:9-10]]

=== Close
--- The faith once delivered [title]
overline: Jude 3
sub: “…earnestly contend for the faith which was once delivered unto the saints.”
point: A line of believers | Not a chain of churches, but believers in every age, saved by grace through faith. [[Hebrews 12:1]]
point: The distinctives are the Bible’s | Not a tradition of men, but what the scriptures teach.
point: The cost was blood | Of whom the world was not worthy. [[Hebrews 11:38]]
point: Our part | Behold, hear, set thine heart, and declare. [[Ezekiel 40:4]]
